<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narmada River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Narmada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Narmada is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, flowing about 1,300 kilometres westwards from Amarkantak in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> through a rift valley between the Vindhya and Satpura ranges to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a>. It has long marked the traditional boundary between northern India and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> halted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> there in the 7th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> regard it as one of the holiest rivers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narmada-river.jpg" alt="Narmada River" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aksveer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narmada_River_Jabalpur_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
